WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.656
Turning a policy on is the moment
a production job can stop,

2
00:00:03.656 --> 00:00:07.412
so the code asks for five things
at once, or the ring stays held.

3
00:00:07.412 --> 00:00:10.224
A D N P five record marks the ring.

4
00:00:10.224 --> 00:00:12.138
The change says allow enforce.

5
00:00:12.138 --> 00:00:16.287
The run names a sign-in summary
whose hash the ring's record cites.

6
00:00:16.287 --> 00:00:18.313
That summary shows the ring ready.

7
00:00:18.313 --> 00:00:21.149
And every earlier ring
is already enforced.

8
00:00:21.149 --> 00:00:24.150
The five conditions are the
program's design guidance.

9
00:00:24.150 --> 00:00:26.512
The evidence comes from
the sign-in reader.

10
00:00:26.512 --> 00:00:29.603
Following Microsoft Learn's
List sign-ins reference,

11
00:00:29.603 --> 00:00:33.889
it reads the beta sign-in list with a
filter for service principal sign-ins,

12
00:00:33.889 --> 00:00:37.824
and sends a header without which
report-only results don't come back.

13
00:00:37.824 --> 00:00:40.847
Each principal gets one of four statuses:

14
00:00:40.847 --> 00:00:44.225
no sign-ins, not evaluated,
would block, or clear.

15
00:00:44.225 --> 00:00:48.000
A ring is ready only when
every principal in it is clear.
